  "research": {
    "title": "Casio DG-20 Digital Guitar",
    "year": "1987",
    "by": "Casio Computer Co.",
    "tags": ["HCI", "Input", "Music", "Musical Instrument", "Switch Matrix"],
    "overview": "The Casio DG-20 Digital Guitar (1987) is a MIDI guitar controller built for consumers. Instead of converting the vibration of plucked strings into pitch through signal processing — the slow, error-prone route of most MIDI guitars — Casio hardwired the instrument's geometry into the electronics. The fretboard is a rubber membrane switch matrix: pressing a string against a fret completes a circuit that is physically wired to a specific MIDI pitch, so fretting a note is a switch closure, not a pitch estimate. The strings are nylon triggers; strumming them fires the fretted note. There is no tracking latency because there is no tracking — the instrument guarantees the pitch by construction.",
    "deepDive": [
      {
        "heading": "The fretboard as switch matrix",
        "text": "Most MIDI guitars read pitch from a vibrating string with a pickup and try to estimate the note, which introduces latency and errors. Casio's answer was to make each fret a switch: every fret contact on the membrane fretboard is electrically mapped to a specific pitch for each string. Fretting is not sensing, it is completing a circuit. The result is a guitar-shaped instrument where the note you fret is guaranteed — a 'hardwired, no-tracking' input philosophy."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Consumer oddity",
        "text": "The DG-20 (and its non-MIDI sibling DG-10) were aimed at hobbyists who wanted a synthesizer but did not want to learn a keyboard. The rubber strings and membrane fretboard made it approachable and cheap, but also distinctly toy-like compared to professional controllers like the museum's SynthAxe. It is the mass-market, membrane-switch end of the same idea: the guitar as a MIDI input device."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Why it matters to HCI",
        "text": "The DG-20 is a clean example of moving computation into the physical structure of an input device. Rather than processing a continuous signal to infer intent, the device re-engineers the input surface so that intent is structurally guaranteed — a switch matrix instead of a pitch tracker. It anticipates a whole family of 'the hardware guarantees the input' instruments."
      }
    ],
